course: dictionary
lesson: 12028
title: A name or a broken word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words you meet in a text are not everyday English words at all. They are names. When you see an unknown word with a capital letter in the middle of a sentence, ask: is this a person's name? «Mia Chon sat at the front of the class.» Here Chon comes after Mia, so it is her family name, not a word with a meaning like happy or fast.
explain: Sometimes a word looks strange because part of it is missing. A text may be cut off, so you only see «chon» at the end of a longer word. Look at the letters before it and the words around it. If you cannot find a clear meaning, it may be a broken word, and you should check the whole sentence or page.
explain: Names can be written in different ways in different families and countries. Chon may also be written Jeon or Chun. The name is the same even when the spelling changes. When a word is a name, you do not need to work out its meaning. You only need to know it names a person.
fact: Chon is a surname and a given name.
fact: Chon is often a Korean surname, and it is also used in some other Asian and Hispanic families.
fact: Chon can also be written Jeon or Chun.
fact: Chon is not a common English word, so it does not have an everyday meaning like happy or fast.
fact: If chon appears alone in a text, check whether it is a name or part of a longer word that was cut off.
exercise: «Mia Chon sat at the front of the class.» What is Chon in this sentence? | A colour | A type of fruit | A word for running fast | *A name, the family name of Mia
exercise: Ms Park read the class list: «Chon, Ellis, Grace, Omar.» What kind of word is Chon? | A verb meaning to jump | An adjective meaning very cold | *A name | A word for a place where you buy food
exercise: Ali found «chon» at the end of a line, but the letters before it were cut off: «...ly chon.» What should he do? | Guess it is a kind of food | Skip the whole book at once | *Check whether it is a name or part of a longer broken word | Decide it means very happy
test: «Chon can also be written Jeon or Chun.» What is this clue telling you? | *The same name can be spelled in different ways | Chon means very old | Chun is a common English word | Jeon is a fast animal
test: «Chon is not a common English word, so Sara checks the name list.» Why does Sara check? | Because chon is a kind of food | Because chon is a question | Because chon is too short to read | *Because chon is a name, not an everyday word
exam: In the story, «Hana Chon is in Year 5.» Is Chon a common English word with an everyday meaning? | Yes, it means happy | Yes, it means a kind of tree | No, it is a verb that means to run | *No, it is a name, so you check whether it is a surname
